  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2607.28757v1</id>
    <title>Partial Derandomization for Leakage-Resilient Shamir's Secret Sharing over Composite Order Fields</title>
    <updated>2026-07-30T18:24:52Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.28757v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.28757v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We make progress on the question of constructing explicit evaluation places for leakage-resilient Shamir's secret sharing, over composite order fields. Previously, Maji et al. (EUROCRYPT 2024) showed that random evaluation places yield Shamir's secret sharing over the composite order field $\mathbb{F}_{p^d}$ that is statistically secure against physical-bit leakage. Later, Nguyen (EUROCRYPT 2025) established a dichotomy that linear code-based secret-sharing scheme over the field $\mathbb{F}_{p^d}$ is either statistically secure or completely insecure against such leakage.
  Building upon Nguyen's dichotomy, we present a partial derandomization of evaluation places, improving upon the Maji et al. result for a restricted regime of parameters. We replace the random choice of $n$ independent evaluation places by the iterates $x_j = Φ^j(x_0)$ of a simple fixed rational function $Φ$, where the initial point $x_0 \in \mathbb{F}_{p^d}^*$ is randomly chosen. The randomness in the evaluation places thus drops from $nd \log p$ bits to $d\log p$ bits. Our construction is valid for the regime $n = O(d/\log_p d)$, and any reconstruction threshold $k \ge 2$; in fact, the scheme attains perfect security (statistical distance exactly zero) against single-block leakage. Our technique is a partial fraction nondegeneracy argument that exploits the distinct poles of the rational iterates.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.IT" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-07-30T18:24:52Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>A preliminary version of this work is due to appear at ITC 2026</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CR"/>
    <author>
      <name>S. Venkitesh</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20173v2</id>
    <title>Secret Sharing Despite Non-Extractable Randomness</title>
    <updated>2026-11-03T04:42:36Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20173v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.20173v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Bosley and Dodis (TCC 2007) proved that a class of imperfect randomness sources that suffices for information-theoretic encryption of messages longer than the logarithm of the key length must admit deterministic extraction of nearly uniform bits, and asked whether the same is true of two-out-of-two secret sharing. Aggarwal, Chung, Obremski, and Ribeiro (TCC 2022) gave a positive answer for schemes that are in addition leakage-resilient or non-malleable; the original question remained open.
  We answer it in the negative. For every $b\ge2$ we construct a class of flat sources on $\{0,1\}^{b+2}$, each of min-entropy at least $b$, together with a perfectly correct and perfectly private two-out-of-two scheme that shares a $b$-bit secret into two $(b+1)$-bit shares using any source in the class, and we show that every deterministic map from $\{0,1\}^{b+2}$ to $\{0,1\}$ is at statistical distance at least $1/6$ from uniform on some source in the class. This implies that sharing a long enough secret does not require extractable randomness. The proof is a short parity argument. The idea of the construction extends to other thresholds: for two-out-of-$n$ and $r$-out-of-$r$ sharing we give perfect schemes with $(b+1)$-bit shares, private for classes of flat sources from which no single bit can be extracted with error below $1/(4n-2)$ and $1/(2(2^r-1))$, respectively.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-14T13:20:25Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Divesh Aggarwal</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
